WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:02.100
- A lot has already been
written about Elon Musk.

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:05.010
He lives his life between the tweets,

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:08.610
upended, reinvented the auto industry,

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:12.120
aerospace, now trying it
with Twitter turned X.

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:14.070
You spent two years shadowing him.

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:16.740
What did you learn that
we didn't already know

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:17.730
about Elon Musk?

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:18.630
- Well, there's certainly

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:21.900
multiple Elon Musks,
multiple personalities.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:24.180
Whether it's a hardcore engineering mode

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:25.140
that you know so well,

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:27.540
where he can figure out
how to do a battery cell,

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:30.030
or more importantly,
figure out how to do steps

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:33.090
on an assembly line that
will make the battery cell.

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:36.270
But also, there's almost
a demon mode of Elon Musk,

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:38.220
where he turns really dark.

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:40.440
And that can be very problematic,

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.930
especially when it came
to acquiring Twitter,

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:45.840
which I think involves more
emotional intelligence.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:47.610
- Are there ground rules,
anything off limits?

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:49.531
Did you sleep on the
factory floor next to him?

00:00:49.531 --> 00:00:50.610
(Walter laughing)

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:53.040
- You know, I slept in Airstream trailers

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:54.810
down in the south tip of Texas

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:57.090
where SpaceX has its launchpad.

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:59.460
I spent a lotta late nights in factories.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:01.590
He put nothing off limits,

00:01:01.590 --> 00:01:03.660
which surprised the heck out of me.

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:07.230
I mean, meetings that were
about forward-looking products,

00:01:07.230 --> 00:01:08.707
everything else, he said,

00:01:08.707 --> 00:01:12.120
"I just wanna surprise you
about the transparency."

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:13.597
And my side of the deal was,

00:01:13.597 --> 00:01:15.630
"Fine, I wanna be everywhere,

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:17.250
but you get no control over the book.

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:19.200
You don't even get to read
it before it comes out."

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:20.317
- You've written a lot of books.

00:01:20.317 --> 00:01:23.280
"Steve Jobs," biography was maybe,

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:24.870
perhaps, an instant classic.

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:27.120
And in that book, you
talk about the challenge

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:28.169
of breaking through

00:01:28.169 --> 00:01:32.160
the Apple co-founder's
reality distortion field.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:33.810
With Musk, you had extraordinary access.

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:37.620
By my count, you talked to more
than 120 people around him,

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:41.370
close deputies, even family
members, his father even.

00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:43.890
Did you worry about that
reality distortion field

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:46.500
that he has, the fact
that he can sometimes be

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:48.900
an unreliable narrator in his own life?

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:51.330
- Well, yes, he can be unreliable,

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:54.150
and his memory is fallible
and he sort of cloaks himself

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:56.670
as a hero in every tale.

00:01:56.670 --> 00:02:00.000
But I didn't feel a
reality distortion field.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.780
He was not, he almost was
unaware of my presence,

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:05.130
it seemed, a lot of the time.

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:07.950
'Cause he'd be being a real jerk to people

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:10.950
at a meeting or blow
up on a walk around one

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:12.510
of the rocket sites.

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:14.160
And I'd think, "Wait a
minute, doesn't he know

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:15.600
I'm taking notes here?"

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:19.980
So it did surprise me
how open and in some ways

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:22.680
not trying to sugar coat things he was.

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:25.650
- You spent a really
significant part of the book

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:28.680
in the beginning talking
about his childhood

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:30.330
and his relationship with his father.

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:33.300
What was the early days of Elon Musk like?

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:36.780
- You know, he was a scrawny
kid, socially awkward,

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:38.970
beaten up on the playground a whole lot,

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:41.130
went to a wilderness camp,

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:43.410
in which, you know, sort of the survival.

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:45.510
And he lost 10 pounds in a week

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:48.450
because they kept beating
him up and taking his food.

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:50.610
But when he went back
to the wilderness camp

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:53.040
a few years later, he had gotten bigger.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:54.907
He had learned a little judo, and he said,

00:02:54.907 --> 00:02:57.120
"I just learned to
punch people in the nose

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:00.120
as hard as I could when
they were being mean to me.

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:01.740
And sometimes they'd beat me up,

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:03.690
but if I punched them
really hard in the nose,

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:04.980
they wouldn't do it again."

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:08.670
You almost see that nowadays,
which is he's pugnacious.

00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:11.970
He sometimes can be so confrontational,

00:03:11.970 --> 00:03:14.640
and when in doubt, he punches in the nose.

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:17.280
- You talk about his
relationship with his father.

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:19.230
His father is almost described

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:21.840
like a Jekyll and Hyde-like character.

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:24.810
Sometimes he can be friendly,
sometimes he can be volatile.

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:26.640
I've never seen him go so in-depth

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:28.440
about that childhood experience.

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:30.690
Why do you think he was
ready to talk about it now?

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:33.510
- It took a long time
for Musk to wanna talk

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:34.770
about his childhood.

00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:37.740
I just sat there, I'd raise
it every now and then,

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:39.240
and sometimes we'd get to a place

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:41.760
where suddenly, he'd start
talking about his father.

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:45.000
And he would go, get teary-eyed at times,

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:47.227
very, very emotional, and just say,

00:03:47.227 --> 00:03:49.380
"You don't know how bad the abuse was."

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:53.310
And after awhile, Elon
just kept opening up

00:03:53.310 --> 00:03:56.160
about the psychological scars he had

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:59.940
when his father would make him
stand in front of his father

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:02.490
and yell at him for more than an hour

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:05.430
and take the sides of the
people who had beaten up Elon

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:06.870
and call Elon stupid.

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:10.230
- You write that, "The
PTSD from his childhood

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:13.500
also instilled in him an
aversion to contentment."

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:16.740
What do you think that
means for the companies

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:17.573
that he runs?

00:04:17.573 --> 00:04:20.340
What does that mean for the
people that work for him

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:22.080
as they try to reach the goals

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:24.030
and sometimes achieve those goals?

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:25.440
- He's addicted to drama.

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:27.090
He's addicted to risk.

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:29.880
And whenever things seem
to be going smoothly,

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:33.210
he almost has a compulsion
to stir things up.

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:36.570
It allows him to become
a disruptive figure,

00:04:36.570 --> 00:04:38.850
but also a difficult leader.

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:41.790
- What do you think was
one of the most surprising

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:44.670
kind of instances of him losing his temper

00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:46.500
with one of his employees?

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:51.500
- Oh, one night in South Texas
at the launchpad of SpaceX,

00:04:51.690 --> 00:04:53.910
it's a Friday night at 10:00 p.m.

00:04:53.910 --> 00:04:55.620
And everything's going smoothly.

00:04:55.620 --> 00:04:57.270
There's no launches being scheduled.

00:04:57.270 --> 00:04:59.700
And he looks and there's
only two people working

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:00.660
at the launchpad.

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:02.880
And all of a sudden, I
see demon mode coming in,

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:05.495
almost like storm clouds
from the Gulf of Mexico.

00:05:05.495 --> 00:05:09.180
And I'm thinking, "Well, it's
Friday night at 10:00 p.m."

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:12.960
And yet, he just reams
out this guy named Andy

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:16.530
who was in charge of the launchpad site.

00:05:16.530 --> 00:05:18.060
And he orders a surge.

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:21.870
He orders 100 people to
come in from different parts

00:05:21.870 --> 00:05:24.630
of SpaceX, from Florida, California,

00:05:24.630 --> 00:05:27.930
so they can all work for 24 hours a day

00:05:27.930 --> 00:05:29.040
getting this thing done,

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:31.110
even though there was no need to.

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:34.350
- What is demon mode in your mind?

00:05:34.350 --> 00:05:37.470
- It's almost like Dr.
Jekyll and Mr. Hyde.

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:40.860
Where a cloud comes over
and he gets into a trance,

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:43.980
and he can just be tough in a cold way.

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:46.530
He never gets really angry,
never gets that physical,

00:05:46.530 --> 00:05:49.740
but coldly brutal to people.

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:54.240
And he almost doesn't remember
afterwards what he's done.

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:57.420
Sometimes I'll say, "Why did
you say that to that person?"

00:05:57.420 --> 00:05:59.100
And he'll look at me blankly,

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:01.260
as if he didn't quite
remember what happened

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:02.638
while he was in demon mode.

00:06:02.638 --> 00:06:03.471
(crowd yelling)

00:06:03.471 --> 00:06:06.570
Now, one of the things that
Claire Boucher, known as Grimes,

00:06:06.570 --> 00:06:08.940
says is, "You don't wanna be around him

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:10.500
when he's in demon mode.

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:13.260
It's really frightening," she said.

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:16.920
But then she added, "But demon
mode is what gets shit done."

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:19.200
- Given all of the stories
about how he treats people,

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:20.970
why do you think he still has the ability

00:06:20.970 --> 00:06:22.890
to attract talent to his companies?

00:06:22.890 --> 00:06:25.320
- I talked to some of the
people on the Autopilot team

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:27.780
at Tesla when they were
doing Optimus, the robot.

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:29.700
And a couple of 'em had quit

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:32.130
because it was just too
brutal dealing with him,

00:06:32.130 --> 00:06:33.750
and they got burned out.

00:06:33.750 --> 00:06:35.100
But then one of 'em was back.

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:35.933
I said, "What happened?"

00:06:35.933 --> 00:06:39.180
He said, "I decided I had a
choice between being burned out

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:42.150
or being bored and I decided
I wanted to be burned out

00:06:42.150 --> 00:06:43.950
because I believed in the mission."

00:06:43.950 --> 00:06:46.920
So some survive, but a whole lot don't.

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:49.860
- Another key one of the ways
of how he tries to manage

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:52.440
or how we kinda tries
to think about the world

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:54.210
is something he calls first principles,

00:06:54.210 --> 00:06:57.480
which is essentially a problem-solving way

00:06:57.480 --> 00:06:59.820
to not take what's been done in the past

00:06:59.820 --> 00:07:01.590
as the way it should
be done in the future.

00:07:01.590 --> 00:07:03.810
As trying to reach the ultimate goal

00:07:03.810 --> 00:07:05.711
of whatever you're setting out,

00:07:05.711 --> 00:07:07.650
kind of getting to the root problem

00:07:07.650 --> 00:07:08.910
and figuring it out from there.

00:07:08.910 --> 00:07:10.080
But that's very abstract.

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:11.400
It's kind of hard to put that,

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:12.870
understand what he means by that,

00:07:12.870 --> 00:07:15.150
but it seems as if by
sitting around with him,

00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:17.160
you've kind of seen the way he's trying

00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:19.890
to impart that decision-making
framework to people

00:07:19.890 --> 00:07:21.630
with something called the algorithm.

00:07:21.630 --> 00:07:22.860
What is the algorithm?

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:25.770
- The algorithm is a five-step process

00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:27.960
for not only making good products

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:31.290
and designing good products,
but manufacturing them.

00:07:31.290 --> 00:07:32.310
And you're right.

00:07:32.310 --> 00:07:34.500
It begins with first principles.

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:37.440
He says, "Question every requirement."

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:39.030
And by first principles,

00:07:39.030 --> 00:07:40.920
he means look down at the physics.

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:44.160
If somebody says, "No, we
can't build it at this price,"

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:46.920
he says, "Tell me how
much the materials cost.

00:07:46.920 --> 00:07:49.980
Tell me exactly what's involved here.

00:07:49.980 --> 00:07:52.650
And then, tell me you can or can't do it."

00:07:52.650 --> 00:07:56.010
When he first was trying
to buy a rocket booster

00:07:56.010 --> 00:07:58.110
in Russia early on,

00:07:58.110 --> 00:08:01.680
and they were charging him
$15 million or something,

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:05.610
on the flight home, he
calculated how much is the cost

00:08:05.610 --> 00:08:08.317
of materials and fuel, and he said,

00:08:08.317 --> 00:08:10.680
"Okay, those are the first principles.

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:15.000
We should be able to make
it at a much cheaper cost."

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:17.077
With the algorithm, he's always saying,

00:08:17.077 --> 00:08:18.960
"Question every requirement.

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:20.940
And then delete, delete, delete."

00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:22.770
So he simplifies things.

00:08:22.770 --> 00:08:25.350
And then, only then do
you start to speed up

00:08:25.350 --> 00:08:28.770
the manufacturing process,
and eventually, automate it.

00:08:28.770 --> 00:08:31.230
One of his big mistakes at Tesla

00:08:31.230 --> 00:08:33.720
was he went to automation too quickly

00:08:33.720 --> 00:08:36.150
before he had deleted every part

00:08:36.150 --> 00:08:37.770
and questioned every requirement.

00:08:37.770 --> 00:08:39.480
- Do you see him moving into a new era

00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:42.510
where he's essentially
seeding a bunch of ideas,

00:08:42.510 --> 00:08:44.130
almost a Thomas Edison character,

00:08:44.130 --> 00:08:45.930
putting money towards bigger ideas

00:08:45.930 --> 00:08:47.340
and letting smart people invent it?

00:08:47.340 --> 00:08:51.162
Or is he gonna kinda keep
control like a Henry Ford

00:08:51.162 --> 00:08:54.660
back in the day of making sure
the Model T was the Model T

00:08:54.660 --> 00:08:56.100
for as long as possible?

00:08:56.100 --> 00:08:59.820
- Well, I think one of his
weaknesses, I'll call it,

00:08:59.820 --> 00:09:01.503
is he really is a control freak.

00:09:03.077 --> 00:09:04.230
You want him to give up control.

00:09:04.230 --> 00:09:05.227
Say of Twitter, you're thinking,

00:09:05.227 --> 00:09:07.800
"Okay, Linda Yaccarino should run it."

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:10.800
But he doesn't really
cede too much control.

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:13.380
Gwynne Shotwell at SpaceX
learns how to deal with him.

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:16.050
He really is focused on the product.

00:09:16.050 --> 00:09:17.850
She gets to run the company.

00:09:17.850 --> 00:09:20.850
But no, I don't see him
giving up a lotta control

00:09:20.850 --> 00:09:23.040
over these companies, even
though they're are now

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:24.150
six or seven of 'em,

00:09:24.150 --> 00:09:26.280
depending on how you
count, that he's running.

00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:29.790
- Are we in the final act
of this kind of narrative?

00:09:29.790 --> 00:09:30.900
Has he peaked?

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:32.523
Is he on the decline?

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:35.310
I would say that, for many years,

00:09:35.310 --> 00:09:38.400
people have predicted his
kinda the epic collapse

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:40.563
of something, and he's been
able to get through it.

00:09:40.563 --> 00:09:42.720
Is this just another example of that?

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:44.040
- Some of 'me are gonna collapse.

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:46.470
It's gonna be like his rocket
ships and everything else.

00:09:46.470 --> 00:09:48.960
He believes you can't get into orbit

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:51.180
without blowing things
up every now and then.

00:09:51.180 --> 00:09:52.830
So there will be debris.

00:09:52.830 --> 00:09:54.570
There will be things blown up.

00:09:54.570 --> 00:09:57.690
But I think, you know,
most things he's done

00:09:57.690 --> 00:09:59.973
keep surprising me as being successful.

